WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.920
Prompt quality should be measured with a test set, not judged from one successful response.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:17.940
A useful test set includes typical inputs, difficult inputs, ambiguous inputs, missing information, long inputs, edge cases, irrelevant text, conflicting text, and inputs that

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.080
should be refused or escalated.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.180
The goal is to understand behavior across the range of work the prompt is expected to handle.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.800
Evaluation criteria should match the task.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:34.100
For extraction, criteria may include field accuracy, missing-field handling, and valid output structure.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:40.480
For summarization, criteria may include completeness, relevance, source support, and length.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:47.660
For classification, criteria may include label accuracy, confidence handling, and consistent treatment of edge cases.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:54.360
Security and privacy criteria may include safe handling of sensitive data and refusal to treat untrusted content as instruction.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:58.660
Automated checks and human evaluation serve different purposes.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:07.280
Automated checks can validate required fields, JSON syntax, known answers, category labels, length limits, or forbidden output patterns.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.260
Human reviewers are often needed for nuance, usefulness, fairness, factual interpretation, and tone.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:18.200
For high-impact workflows, human review may be part of the product design, not an afterthought.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.380
Regression testing protects working behavior over time.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:31.520
When the prompt, model, retrieval system, tool schema, application configuration, or source corpus changes, rerun representative tests and compare results.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.700
A change that improves one example may break another.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.440
Track prompt versions, test results, known limitations, and release decisions.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:44.660
Prompt engineering is easier to maintain when evaluation is repeatable.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.440
Evaluation should include failure expectations.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:53.820
Some inputs should produce an answer, some should ask for clarification, and some should be refused or escalated.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:59.400
For example, a support workflow may need to escalate missing account context instead of inventing it.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.540
A research workflow may need to say that sources do not support a claim.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.440
These outcomes are part of quality, not failures of helpfulness.
